THE FERMI ENERGY OF A MONOVALENT METAL SUCH AS COPPER CAN BE ESTIMATED USING THE FREE‑ELECTRON MODEL:
\[
E_F=\frac{\hbar^{2}}{2m}\left(3\pi^{2}n\right)^{2/3}
\]

FOR COPPER:
- DENSITY \(\rho = 8.96\ \text{g cm}^{-3}\)
- ATOMIC MASS \(M = 63.5\ \text{g mol}^{-1}\)
- ONE CONDUCTION ELECTRON PER ATOM (VALENCE = 1)

NUMBER DENSITY OF ELECTRONS:
\[
n = \frac{N_A \rho}{M}= \frac{6.022\times10^{23}\times8.96}{63.5}
   \approx 8.5\times10^{22}\ \text{cm}^{-3}=8.5\times10^{28}\ \text{m}^{-3}
\]

INSERTING CONSTANTS:
\[
\hbar = 1.055\times10^{-34}\ \text{J·s},\quad 
m = 9.11\times10^{-31}\ \text{kg}
\]

\[
E_F = \frac{(1.055\times10^{-34})^{2}}{2(9.11\times10^{-31})}
      \left(3\pi^{2}\times8.5\times10^{28}\right)^{2/3}
      \approx 1.13\times10^{-18}\ \text{J}
\]

CONVERT TO ELECTRONVOLTS \((1\ \text{eV}=1.602\times10^{-19}\ \text{J})\):
\[
E_F \approx \frac{1.13\times10^{-18}}{1.602\times10^{-19}}
      \approx 7.0\ \text{eV}
\]

