in the centre‑of‑mass frame the total energy before the annihilation is the sum of the rest energies of the proton and antiproton (each ≈938 MeV) plus the tiny kinetic energy of the slow antiproton, which we can neglect. therefore

\[
E_{\text{initial}}\simeq 2\,m_p c^2\approx 2\times 938\ \text{MeV}=1876\ \text{MeV}.
\]

after the annihilation we have two particles of type A and two of its antiparticle, each of rest mass \(m_Ac^2=300\ \text{MeV}\). the total rest‑mass energy of the four particles is

\[
E_{\text{rest}}=4\,m_Ac^2 = 4\times 300\ \text{MeV}=1200\ \text{MeV}.
\]

the difference between the initial total energy and the total rest energy appears as kinetic energy of the four A particles. because the final state is symmetric, the four particles share the kinetic energy equally, so each particle has kinetic energy

\[
K = \frac{E_{\text{initial}}-E_{\text{rest}}}{4}
   = \frac{1876-1200}{4}\ \text{MeV}
   = \frac{676}{4}\ \text{MeV}
   \approx 169\ \text{MeV}.
\]

for a particle of mass 300 MeV the relativistic relation between kinetic energy \(K\) and speed \(v\) is

\[
K = (\gamma-1)m_Ac^2,\qquad \gamma = \frac{1}{\sqrt{1-\beta^2}},\;\; \beta = v/c .
\]

solving for \(\beta\),

\[
\gamma = 1 + \frac{K}{m_Ac^2}
       = 1 + \frac{169}{300}
       \approx 1.563,
\]

\[
\beta = \sqrt{1-\frac{1}{\gamma^2}}
      = \sqrt{1-\frac{1}{(1.563)^2}}
      \approx \sqrt{1-0.409}
      \approx \sqrt{0.591}
      \approx 0.77 .
\]

however this value corresponds to the kinetic energy assigned to each particle if the four particles share the kinetic energy equally. in fact the annihilation produces two \(A^+\) and two \(A^-\) moving back‑to‑back in pairs; momentum conservation forces each pair to carry half of the available kinetic energy, so each individual particle gets twice the kinetic energy used above:

\[
K_{\text{particle}} = \frac{E_{\text{initial}}-E_{\text{rest}}}{2}
                    = \frac{676}{2}\ \text{MeV}
                    = 338\ \text{MeV}.
\]

now

\[
\gamma = 1 + \frac{338}{300}
